repo instead of something else:

.. click:example::

    @cli.command()
    @click.argument('src')
    @click.argument('dest', required=False)
    @pass_repo
    def clone(repo, src, dest):
        pass

Ensuring Objects
----------------

Our decorator is all nice and generic but there's one problem: it stops with
an error when no ``Repo`` object was created yet.  As the example above
shows, that object is created by the root command which means that the
``clone`` command can only ever be invoked through it.  A command that shows
up in the help page should of course also work when it is called on its own.

To make that possible the factory function can be told to ensure the object
exists by passing ``ensure=True``.  If no object of that type was stored on
any of the contexts yet, a new empty one is created for the current context
and remembered as :attr:`~Context.obj` so that it can be used from there on.
When the root command was executed before, its own object is found and used
like always:

.. click:example::

    pass_repo = click.make_pass_decorator(Repo, ensure=True)

.. click:example::

    @cli.command()
    @click.argument('src')
    @click.argument('dest', required=False)
    @pass_repo
    def clone(repo, src, dest):
        click.echo('Clone from %s to %s' % (repo.home, dest))

.. click:run::

    invoke(clone, ['repo1', 'repo2'])

